From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 02:13:22 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Resolve each chunk once for grass spread

BlockGrass.updateTick is the busiest random tick on an overworld. Every read in
it hashed the chunk coordinates again, and the spread probes read the same column
twice in a row: getType for the block above, then getTypeIfLoaded for the block
itself, two lookups of one chunk. A tick did on the order of fourteen.

The probe offsets are +-1 in x and z, so a tick touches at most four chunks and
usually one. The source chunk is resolved once up front - the block being ticked
lives in a loaded chunk by construction - and each probe column once, which covers
every block read in the method. This is the shape patch 0175 used for the leaf
decay scan.

The probe position is no longer built before the tests that throw it away. Only
the isLightLevel call still needs one, and the chain reaches that last, so the
common path where the probe is not dirt now allocates nothing.

Behaviour is unchanged. The three offsets are drawn in the same order the
BlockPosition constructor drew them, so the random sequence is identical. The two
reads swap order, which is not observable - both are pure, and the only caller
that could have seen a difference is a probe in an unloaded chunk, which used to
read air for the block above and then return on the null; it still returns, one
lookup earlier. getType's decorating() branch is not lost: decorating() is true
only on the decoration worker thread, and a grass tick reaching that thread is
recorded by forceBlockUpdateTick and replayed on the server thread instead.

Measured with JFR on a fixed route, comparing shares within each run because the
block tick sweep's share of the thread swings between 16% and 26% run to run and
totals across runs are not comparable. Inside BlockGrass.updateTick's own samples:

  World.getChunkAtWorldCoords     32.4% -> 8.6%
  LongObjectHashMap.get           29.1% -> 8.0%
  World.getChunkIfLoaded          24.6% -> 9.7%
  World.getType                   29.1% -> gone
  ChunkProviderServer.getOrCreateChunk  8.4% -> gone

The lookups are demonstrably gone. The end to end effect is about 1% of the
server thread, which is below what the saturating TPS harness can resolve at its
0.9% noise floor, so this is not claimed as a TPS win.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/BlockGrass.java b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/BlockGrass.java
index 33445a3784fcaf2232cbe902bf96d4e013d44f1b..53d6dc8f26a8fa175044fbee8339a6ce4ef7d27f 100644
--- a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/BlockGrass.java
+++ b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/BlockGrass.java
@@ -30,8 +30,32 @@ public class BlockGrass extends Block implements IBlockFragilePlantElement {
 
     public void updateTick(World world, BlockPosition blockposition, IBlockData iblockdata, Random random) {
         if (!world.isClientSide) {
+            // Athena start - resolve each chunk once instead of once per read.
+            //
+            // Grass spread is the busiest random tick on an overworld: a profile of the block tick
+            // sweep put BlockGrass.updateTick at 4.1% of the server thread, and 42% of that was
+            // inside getChunkAtWorldCoords - LongObjectHashMap.get alone was the leaf of a quarter
+            // of its samples. Every read here hashed the chunk coordinates again, and the spread
+            // probes read the same column twice in a row.
+            //
+            // The probe offsets are +-1 in x and z, so a tick touches at most four chunks and
+            // usually one. Resolving the source chunk up front and each probe's chunk once covers
+            // every read below. This is the same shape as the leaf decay scan.
+            int x = blockposition.getX();
+            int y = blockposition.getY();
+            int z = blockposition.getZ();
+            int chunkX = x >> 4;
+            int chunkZ = z >> 4;
+            Chunk chunk = world.getChunkIfLoaded(chunkX, chunkZ);
+
+            if (chunk == null) {
+                // The block being random-ticked lives in a loaded chunk by construction, so this
+                // is unreachable; bail rather than fall through to a null dereference.
+                return;
+            }
+            // Athena end
             int lightLevel = -1; // PandaSpigot
-            if (world.getType(blockposition.up()).getBlock().getLightOpacity() > 2 && (lightLevel = world.getLightLevel(blockposition.up())) < 4) { // PandaSpigot - Move light check to end to avoid unneeded light lookups
+            if (world.getTypeIfLoaded(x, y + 1, z, chunk).getBlock().getLightOpacity() > 2 && (lightLevel = world.getLightLevel(blockposition.up())) < 4) { // PandaSpigot - Move light check to end to avoid unneeded light lookups // Athena - the block above shares this chunk
                 // CraftBukkit start
                 // world.setTypeUpdate(blockposition, Blocks.DIRT.getBlockData());
                 org.bukkit.World bworld = world.getWorld();
@@ -53,20 +77,35 @@ public class BlockGrass extends Block implements IBlockFragilePlantElement {
                 if (lightLevel >= 9) {
                 // PandaSpigot end
                     for (int i = 0; i < Math.min(4, Math.max(20, (int) (4 * 100F / world.growthOdds))); ++i) { // Spigot
-                        BlockPosition blockposition1 = blockposition.add(random.nextInt(3) - 1, random.nextInt(5) - 3, random.nextInt(3) - 1);
-                        Block block = world.getType(blockposition1.up()).getBlock();
+                        // Athena - the three offsets are drawn in the same order the BlockPosition
+                        // constructor used to draw them, so the random sequence is unchanged.
+                        int x1 = x + random.nextInt(3) - 1;
+                        int y1 = y + random.nextInt(5) - 3;
+                        int z1 = z + random.nextInt(3) - 1;
+                        // Athena - one lookup for the probe column, reused for both reads below.
+                        // A probe outside a loaded chunk used to read air for the block above and
+                        // then return on the null below; it still returns, one lookup earlier.
+                        Chunk probe = (x1 >> 4) == chunkX && (z1 >> 4) == chunkZ
+                                ? chunk
+                                : world.getChunkIfLoaded(x1 >> 4, z1 >> 4);
                         // PandaSpigot start
-                        IBlockData iblockdata1 = world.getTypeIfLoaded(blockposition1);
+                        IBlockData iblockdata1 = world.getTypeIfLoaded(x1, y1, z1, probe);
                         if (iblockdata1 == null) {
                             return;
                         }
 
-                        if (iblockdata1.getBlock() == Blocks.DIRT && iblockdata1.get(BlockDirt.VARIANT) == BlockDirt.EnumDirtVariant.DIRT && block.getLightOpacity() <= 2 && world.isLightLevel(blockposition1.up(), 4)) { // Move last check before isLightLevel to avoid unneeded light checks
+                        Block block = world.getTypeIfLoaded(x1, y1 + 1, z1, probe).getBlock(); // Athena - same chunk as the probe
+
+                        // Athena - the probe position used to be built before any of these tests
+                        // and thrown away by the first one that failed, which is nearly all of
+                        // them. The only survivor is the position isLightLevel needs, and that is
+                        // now built where the chain reaches it.
+                        if (iblockdata1.getBlock() == Blocks.DIRT && iblockdata1.get(BlockDirt.VARIANT) == BlockDirt.EnumDirtVariant.DIRT && block.getLightOpacity() <= 2 && world.isLightLevel(new BlockPosition(x1, y1 + 1, z1), 4)) { // Move last check before isLightLevel to avoid unneeded light checks
                         // PandaSpigot end
                             // CraftBukkit start
                             // world.setTypeUpdate(blockposition1, Blocks.GRASS.getBlockData());
                             org.bukkit.World bworld = world.getWorld();
-                            BlockState blockState = bworld.getBlockAt(blockposition1.getX(), blockposition1.getY(), blockposition1.getZ()).getState();
+                            BlockState blockState = bworld.getBlockAt(x1, y1, z1).getState(); // Athena - scalar
                             blockState.setType(CraftMagicNumbers.getMaterial(Blocks.GRASS));
 
                             BlockSpreadEvent event = new BlockSpreadEvent(blockState.getBlock(), bworld.getBlockAt(blockposition.getX(), blockposition.getY(), blockposition.getZ()), blockState);
